11. In order to drive home its case under sections 498A and 304 IPC, the prosecution has to prove that the victim was treated with cruelty as defined under section 498A and that such a treatment was with a view to seek dowry and as regards section 304B IPC is concerned the prosecution has to establish the following facts:
i) the death of a woman must have been caused by burns or bodily injury or otherwise than under normal circumstances;
ii) such death must have occurred within seven years of her marriage;
State Vs. Amod Kumar Mishra and Ors. 8 of 29 iii) the woman must have been subjected to cruelty or harassment by her husband or by relatives of her husband;
iv) such cruelty or harassment must be for or in connection with demand for dowry;
v) such cruelty or harassment is shown to have been meted out to the woman soon before her death.

In Kans Raj v. State of Punjab and Ors.
MANU/SC/0296/2000 : AIR 2000 SC 2324 the Hon'ble Supreme Court, inter alia, observed as under;
In cases where such accusations are State Vs. Amod Kumar Mishra and Ors. 27 of 29 made, the overt acts attributed to persons other than husband are required to be proved beyond reasonable doubt.
